---
name: task-learning
description: Close the learning loop at handoff, before landing or closing a ship or scout, after an external merge, and when a later task supplies evidence for a linked prediction. Use existing handback and bounded usage evidence; no transcript audit.
---

# task-learning

You own the integrator check. The Crew owns its repository's pre-handoff learning. Keep one record at `/ws/.mate/projects/shop/mate/learnings/<crew>.md`, sourced to the task, repository, delivered revision (or scout report), and review findings. You may write this record; repository changes still go through a Crew.

## Check once per evidence revision

Read the handback/report's Learning result and any prediction links supplied in its brief. Reuse the existing record for the same revision and review evidence; repeated wake/merge events do not start another retrospective. A fix round checks only new findings. An external merge catches up here before teardown; a closed Crew's retained report is sufficient when its worktree is gone.

`/usr/local/bin/mate land` writes the record's head for a ship whose record is still `Learning: pending`: the source line, the hand-back's own `Learning:` line (or your `--learning "<line>"`) and an empty `## Predictions`. That is the record for a task with no avoidable obstacle; a finding of yours, or a prediction, is written here before you land, and `land` then keeps your record. The app writes the crew's final numbers into the record itself: at `crew stop`, `/usr/local/bin/mate merge`, a console stop or merge, and the budget's hard-ceiling stop it fills `## Usage (app)` - token buckets, model calls, context, timing lanes - in `usage --why`'s words, or `unknown` with the reason, and starts the record with `Learning: pending` when you have not. Leave that section to the app and never rewrite it. Before landing, run `/usr/local/bin/mate usage shop <crew> --why` once only when a finding needs a number to decide; mark such open-task snapshots provisional. If usage is absent, stale or fails, record unknown and continue from the handback; do not reindex, poll, open transcripts or launch a scout merely to fill learning. Reviewer usage is separate: name the scope of each number; consult a named reviewer's bounded page only when the review evidence warrants it, at most two.

Elapsed task age includes idle time. Prompt and tool elapsed intervals overlap, tool invocation totals may include parallel work, and decision/handback waits are distinct from execution. Unallocated time is not measured model latency. Never sum these lanes or infer active work from age minus waits. Use the measured work-category time to identify slow checks; use review findings and fix rounds to assess rework. Missing timing stays unknown.

## Route a concrete finding

Use only evidence already collected. Identify at most three themes: missing locator, repeated reading, unclear scope, repeated failed checks, polling, or rework. High cache-read or a large inspect category alone is not proof of wasted work. With no avoidable obstacle, record `Learning: none` and finish; an incomplete measurement does not prove efficiency.

- A Crew already corrected a repo doc or friction entry: keep its path and evidence pointer.
- A briefing/coordination correction: record it in your lesson with `stow`, with this record as source.
- A repo correction still needed: record its owner and route through an authorised task or proposal. After merge, use a new branch, never rewrite the landed work.
- Product follow-up stays in the tracker/backlog with an owner; learning never turns failed acceptance into a pass.

A missing Crew learning result is caught up from existing evidence in your record. Do not relaunch a Crew just to add a ceremonial field. Never claim a repo correction completed when it only exists in your memory.

## Make the next task test it

Each proposed efficiency change names a prediction, baseline source and the next relevant area/task. Put a short sourced pointer in PROJECT.md or a lesson (`stow`), then carry that pointer and relevant known facts into the next matching brief (`new-task`). Keep the full record out of always-loaded memory.

Compare only tasks with comparable scope, size, model/effort, environment and measurement coverage. Judge tokens, time and acceptance/review quality together. A prompt edit or merge alone proves no saving. Withdraw or revise an ineffective proposal; standing rules need repeated evidence or a demonstrated mechanical cause.

A prediction is one numbered item under `## Predictions` of the record, `1. <claim>; baseline <source>; next check <task or area> — outcome: open`; `/usr/local/bin/mate learnings open shop` lists every open one and `new-task` carries the matching ones into a brief as `Prediction <crew> <id>: ...` lines. For every such line in this task's brief, close it now with this task's evidence: `/usr/local/bin/mate learnings close shop <crew> <id> improved|unchanged|inconclusive|not comparable --evidence "<task/revision: what it showed>"`. A prediction the brief carried and this record does not close is a missing result. A prediction closed `unchanged` twice is withdrawn: `stow` drops the lesson it came from.

## Result

Keep the record within 40 lines: source task/revision/reviews and observation time; bounded metrics with scope and unknowns; `Learning: none | captured | follow-up`; up to three evidence → destination/owner → prediction/next-check lines; a `## Predictions` section with one numbered item per prediction, `outcome: open`, or none when nothing is predicted; the app's `## Usage (app)` left as it is. `captured` means routing is complete, not that a saving is proven. Finish when every finding has a durable destination or owned follow-up. Return to delivery; full cross-task token reviews remain captain-requested work.
